	"identifying_description": null,
	"web_text": "Jessica Stockholder is known for colorful “situations” made up of everyday elements such as thrift-store furniture, store-bought goods, textiles, yarn, and building supplies. Frequently referencing craft traditions, her ensembles occupy a space between painting, sculpture and installation. The weaving of Square Dance appropriates the God’s eye, a ritual object common to the native people of the Southwestern United States and Northwestern Mexico that provides spiritual protection and guidance. The weaving patterns have roots in the nieríka, or votive yarn paintings, of the Wixáritari (Huichol) that gained international popularity beginning in the 1950s.",
	"didactic_label": "Jessica Stockholder is known for colorful “situations” made up of everyday elements such as thrift-store furniture, store-bought goods, textiles, yarn, and building supplies. Frequently referencing crafttraditions, her ensembles occupy a space between painting, sculpture and installation. The weaving of Square Dance appropriates the God’s eye, a ritual object common to the native people of the Southwestern United States and Northwestern Mexico that provides spiritual protection and guidance. The weaving patterns have roots in the nieríka, or votive yarn paintings, of the Wixáritari (Huichol) that gained international popularity beginning in the 1950s.",
